multichoice[20]{tags,text,exhibit,correct,w1,w2,w3,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.1|objective:cisco:300-435:1.1|topic:yang|topic:openconfig","A team wants one gNMI path syntax and one data model that work on NX-OS switches and on equipment from other vendors. Which model family fits best?",,"OpenConfig models, which are vendor-neutral and written by network operators","Native models such as Cisco-NX-OS-device, which cover every NX-OS feature but only NX-OS","DME managed-object paths, which are shared by the operating systems of all vendors","IETF models only, because NX-OS rejects any other model over gNMI","OpenConfig is vendor-neutral, and NX-OS exposes it once feature openconfig is enabled. Native models give the widest NX-OS coverage but are vendor-specific, DME paths exist only on Cisco platforms, and NX-OS gNMI supports OpenConfig and native models, not only IETF ones.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.2|topic:aci","An engineer migrates 40 VLANs from a classic data center into ACI in network-centric mode. How are the VLANs normally mapped?",,"Each VLAN becomes one bridge domain with one EPG, with permissive forwarding such as an unenforced VRF or permit-any contracts at first","All 40 VLANs share one bridge domain and one EPG so that contracts are never needed","Each VLAN becomes its own tenant so that policy is isolated per VLAN","Each VLAN becomes a contract, and EPGs are created later for each application tier","Network-centric mode keeps the existing design: one VLAN equals one bridge domain equals one EPG, usually with permit-any behavior so that nothing changes for the servers. Application-centric designs group endpoints by application tier instead, and tenants or contracts do not represent VLANs.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.3|topic:dpu","Which statement best describes the role of a data processing unit (DPU) in a data center switch or server?",,"It offloads network, security or storage services onto dedicated programmable processing, freeing the main CPU or switch ASIC","It replaces the spine layer by forwarding traffic between leaf switches","It stores the startup configuration so that a switch can boot without flash storage","It runs the routing protocols for the whole fabric on behalf of every switch","A DPU is a programmable processor placed beside the host CPU or switch ASIC to run infrastructure services such as stateful filtering, encryption and telemetry. It does not replace a fabric layer, hold the startup configuration, or act as a fabric-wide control plane.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.1|objective:cisco:350-901:2.1|objective:cisco:350-901:2.3|topic:gitops|topic:iac","Which statement best describes GitOps for a data center network?",,"The desired state is kept in a Git repository, changes arrive through reviewed merge requests, and automation deploys the repository state to the devices","Engineers change running configurations directly and later copy the result into Git as a backup","Git stores only device software images, and a pipeline downloads them on demand","A scheduler pushes random CLI commands to detect which devices are out of sync","GitOps treats the repository as the single source for intended state, with review before merge and automated deployment afterwards. Copying changes into Git after the fact reverses that flow, and storing images or pushing random commands has nothing to do with declared state.",2,1
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.2|objective:cisco:300-435:3.3|topic:jinja2","With vlans = [{id: 10, name: web, active: true}, {id: 20, name: db, active: false}, {id: 30, name: app, active: true}], which lines does the template in the exhibit render, ignoring blank lines?","```jinja\n{% for vlan in vlans if vlan.active %}\nvlan {{ vlan.id }}\n  name {{ vlan.name | upper }}\n{% endfor %}\n```","vlan 10 / name WEB / vlan 30 / name APP","vlan 10 / name WEB / vlan 20 / name DB / vlan 30 / name APP","vlan 10 / name web / vlan 30 / name app","vlan 20 / name DB","The inline filter on the for loop keeps only items whose active attribute is true, so VLAN 20 is skipped. The upper filter turns each remaining name into capital letters, so the lowercase names do not appear.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.3|objective:cisco:350-901:1.1|objective:cisco:300-435:3.4|topic:ansible|topic:ndfc","Which inventory settings let Ansible manage a Nexus Dashboard Fabric Controller through its REST API with the cisco.dcnm collection?",,"ansible_connection: ansible.netcommon.httpapi with ansible_network_os: cisco.dcnm.dcnm","ansible_connection: ansible.netcommon.network_cli with ansible_network_os: cisco.nxos.nxos","ansible_connection: ansible.netcommon.netconf with ansible_network_os: cisco.dcnm.dcnm","ansible_connection: local with ansible_network_os: cisco.ios.ios","The cisco.dcnm collection talks to the controller's REST API through the httpapi connection plugin, and its network OS value is cisco.dcnm.dcnm. network_cli with cisco.nxos.nxos manages a single switch over SSH, the controller has no NETCONF interface for these modules, and cisco.ios.ios targets IOS devices.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.4|objective:cisco:350-901:1.2|topic:terraform|topic:nxapi","A Terraform configuration manages NX-OS switches with the CiscoDevNet/nxos provider. What must be enabled on each switch for the provider to work?",,"feature nxapi, because the provider calls NX-API REST","feature netconf, because the provider sends NETCONF requests","feature grpc, because the provider uses gNMI Set","feature bash-shell, because Terraform runs inside the switch","The nxos provider communicates through NX-API REST, so feature nxapi is required. Terraform runs on a workstation or pipeline runner, not on the switch, and the provider does not use NETCONF or gNMI.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.5|objective:cisco:350-901:1.2|topic:terraform|topic:troubleshooting","A pipeline run of terraform apply fails with the output in the exhibit. The earlier run on build-07 crashed. What should the engineer do?","```text\nError: Error acquiring the state lock\n\nLock Info:\n  ID:        5c7a1f0e-3b2d-4c8e-9a11-0d2f6b7e8a90\n  Operation: OperationTypeApply\n  Who:       ci-runner@build-07\n```","Confirm that no apply is still running, then release the lock with terraform force-unlock and the lock ID","Delete the state file so that Terraform starts again from an empty state","Run terraform apply with -auto-approve so that the lock is ignored","Change the provider version, because the provider creates the lock","A crashed run can leave the lock behind. After checking that nobody is applying, terraform force-unlock with the ID removes it. Deleting the state would lose track of the managed objects, -auto-approve only skips the confirmation prompt, and the backend creates the lock, not the provider.",4,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.1|objective:cisco:300-435:2.2|topic:ncclient|topic:netconf","An engineer connects ncclient to a Nexus 9000 with manager.connect(host=ip, port=830, username=u, password=p, hostkey_verify=False, device_params={'name': 'nexus'}) and gets connection refused. Which NX-OS command is most likely missing?",,"feature netconf, which starts the NETCONF agent listening on port 830","feature nxapi, which opens the NETCONF subsystem on port 830","feature grpc, which listens on port 830 for gNMI","feature bash-shell, which is required before any SSH subsystem works","On NX-OS the NETCONF agent starts when feature netconf is enabled and listens on port 830. NX-API serves HTTP(S), the gRPC agent defaults to port 50051, and the Bash shell is unrelated to NETCONF.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.2|objective:cisco:300-435:2.5|topic:poap|topic:day-0","Which DHCP options does a POAP-capable Nexus switch rely on to find and fetch its provisioning script?",,"Option 66 or 150 for the script server, and option 67 for the script file name","Option 43 for the controller address, and option 60 for the vendor class","Option 121 for static routes, and option 15 for the domain name","Option 6 for DNS servers, and option 42 for NTP servers","The DHCP offer gives the switch an address, the server that holds the script (option 66, or Cisco's option 150 with a list of addresses) and the script name (option 67). Options 43 and 60 belong to controller-style discovery, and the others do not carry the script location.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.3|objective:cisco:300-435:2.7|topic:bash|topic:on-box","Which configuration command lets an administrator open the Linux Bash shell on a Nexus switch with run bash?",,"feature bash-shell","feature scheduler","feature nxapi","feature telemetry","The Bash shell is disabled by default and is enabled with feature bash-shell. The scheduler runs jobs at set times, NX-API serves HTTP requests and telemetry streams data, so none of them give shell access.",1,1
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.4|topic:ndfc|topic:templates","In Nexus Dashboard Fabric Controller, how do templates and policies relate?",,"A template is a reusable definition with variables, and a policy is an instance of a template with values that is applied to one or more switches","A policy defines the variables, and a template is the deployed copy on the switch","Templates apply only to VRFs, and policies apply only to interfaces","Both are Python scripts that the controller copies to bootflash and runs on the switch","A template holds the CLI content and the variables it needs. A policy is created from a template with specific values and is attached to switches, so one template can drive many policies. They are not Python scripts and are not limited to one object type.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.6|topic:nxapi","A script sends five configuration commands through NX-API CLI. If any command fails, none of the five should stay applied. Which request setting provides that behavior?",,"Type cli_conf with the rollback option set to rollback-on-error","Type cli_show with chunk set to 1","Type cli_conf with output_format set to xml","Type bash with sid set to eoc","NX-API CLI configuration requests accept an error action: stop-on-error, continue-on-error or rollback-on-error. Rollback-on-error undoes the commands already applied. cli_show is for show commands, output_format only chooses XML or JSON, and sid and chunk relate to chunked responses.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.1|objective:cisco:300-435:4.2|objective:cisco:350-901:2.4|topic:cml|topic:simulation","A team wants to test a VXLAN EVPN automation change against NX-OS behavior before it touches production. Which approach fits best?",,"Run the change against virtual Nexus nodes in a simulation such as Cisco Modeling Labs, then promote it through the pipeline","Apply it to production leaf switches during a maintenance window and watch the logs","Test only the Jinja2 rendering, because a correct template proves that the device will accept it","Ping between hosts on the production network after the change","A simulation gives realistic device behavior with no production risk, and it can be rebuilt for every pipeline run. Rendering checks catch template errors but not device behavior, and testing on production is what the simulation is meant to avoid.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.2|objective:cisco:350-901:3.4|topic:pyats|topic:genie","An engineer runs pyats learn bgp before a change and again after it, then runs pyats diff on the two results. What does the diff show?",,"Which parts of the learned BGP state changed between the two snapshots","Whether the configuration syntax of the change was valid","Which users typed commands on the switch during the change","The differences between the running and startup configuration","learn builds a structured model of a feature from device output, so diffing two models shows what changed in the feature state. It does not validate syntax, record users, or compare configuration files.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.3|objective:cisco:350-901:3.1|objective:cisco:300-435:4.5|topic:telemetry|topic:mdt","In NX-OS model-driven telemetry, which object defines what data is streamed?",,"The sensor group, which holds the sensor paths","The destination group, which holds the receiver addresses","The gRPC trustpoint, which holds the certificate","The VRF, which holds the interfaces to sample","A sensor group lists the paths, which is the data. A destination group lists where to send it, and the subscription ties the two together with a sample interval. Certificates and VRFs affect transport, not content.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.4|objective:cisco:300-435:4.5|objective:cisco:350-901:3.1|topic:telemetry|topic:grpc","On NX-OS, which feature enables dial-out streaming telemetry, and which enables the gNMI service for dial-in subscriptions?",,"feature telemetry for dial-out, and feature grpc for the gNMI dial-in service","feature nxapi for dial-out, and feature grpc for NETCONF","feature grpc for dial-out, and feature telemetry for gNMI dial-in","feature bash-shell for both","Dial-out subscriptions need feature telemetry, where the switch connects to the receiver. gNMI dial-in needs feature grpc, where the collector connects to the switch. NX-API and the Bash shell are not telemetry features.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.5|objective:cisco:350-901:2.6|topic:source-of-truth|topic:netbox","A pipeline configures leaf switches from a network source of truth such as NetBox. Which workflow follows best practice?",,"Read the intended data from the source of truth, render it into templates, deploy it, and compare device state with the intended data to detect drift","Change the devices by hand, then copy the final values back into the source of truth","Collect the running configuration of every switch and treat it as the intended state","Keep a separate copy of the VLAN list in every tool so that each can work alone","The source of truth holds intent, and everything else is derived from it, so drift is a difference from intent. Treating running state as intent, or updating the record after the fact, makes the record follow the devices instead of leading them.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:5.1|objective:cisco:300-435:5.2|objective:cisco:350-901:4.1|objective:cisco:350-901:4.5|topic:ai|topic:code-validation","An engineer asks an AI assistant for an Ansible playbook that creates VLANs on NX-OS. Which practice best controls the risk of invented module parameters?",,"Check the generated tasks against the collection's module documentation and run them in a lab or simulation first","Run the playbook on production leaf switches with --diff to see whether it works","Ask the assistant whether it is sure about the parameters","Skip testing, since AI-generated code is peer reviewed by the model itself","Generated code can contain parameters that do not exist, so it has to be checked against authoritative documentation and tested somewhere safe. Production testing is risky, and the model's own confidence is not evidence.",4,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:5.2|objective:cisco:300-435:5.3|objective:cisco:350-901:4.2|topic:ai|topic:ai-security","An AI agent has a tool that returns show running-config from any switch to a hosted language model. What is the main risk, and which control addresses it?",,"Secrets in the configuration can reach the model provider, so redact sensitive lines and return only the fields needed","Higher switch CPU load, so add a cache in front of the tool","Model drift, so retrain the model every night","Slower routing convergence, so lower the protocol timers","Whatever a tool returns is sent to the model, and configurations contain passwords, keys and community strings. Data minimization and redaction address that. CPU load, drift and convergence are different concerns.",4,2

multiselect[20]{tags,text,exhibit,c1,c2,c3,w1,w2,w3,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.4|topic:gnmi","Which two operations are RPCs defined by the gNMI specification itself rather than by gNOI or NETCONF? (Choose two.)",,"Set, which applies updates, replaces and deletes to paths","Subscribe, which streams or polls updates for paths",,"System.Reboot, which belongs to gNOI","File.Put, which belongs to gNOI","commit, which belongs to NETCONF","gNMI defines Capabilities, Get, Set and Subscribe. Rebooting and file transfer are gNOI services, and commit is a NETCONF operation.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.4|topic:gnoi","Which two tasks belong to gNOI rather than to gNMI? (Choose two.)",,"Rebooting a device or switching its control processor (System service)","Transferring a file to or from the device (File service)",,"Streaming interface counters every 10 seconds","Listing the models and encodings that the target supports","Replacing a subtree of configuration","gNOI defines operational services such as System (reboot, ping, traceroute) and File (get, put, remove). Streaming, capability discovery and configuration changes are gNMI functions.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.2|topic:aci","Which two things does an EPG need so that its endpoints can exchange traffic with endpoints in a different EPG in an enforced VRF? (Choose two.)",,"An association with a bridge domain, which gives the EPG its Layer 2 context and its VRF","A provided or consumed contract that permits the traffic",,"A static route inside the bridge domain","A port channel to every leaf switch","A syslog destination group","Every EPG belongs to one bridge domain, and in an enforced VRF traffic between EPGs flows only when a contract permits it. Static routes, port channels and syslog settings have no role in whitelisting traffic between EPGs.",2,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.1|objective:cisco:350-901:2.1|objective:cisco:350-901:2.3|topic:gitops","Which two practices belong to a GitOps workflow for network configuration? (Choose two.)",,"Merge requests are reviewed and approved before the main branch changes","A pipeline validates and deploys what is in the repository to the devices",,"Engineers fix devices in the CLI and update the repository later","The repository stores only rendered output and never the source data","Deployments are started by hand from a laptop with no record","GitOps depends on review before merge and automated deployment from the repository. Fixing devices first reverses the flow, and untracked manual deployments leave no audit trail. Keeping only rendered output loses the data that the output was built from.",2,1
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.2|objective:cisco:300-435:3.3|topic:jinja2","A template must list only the interfaces whose role is access, in name order, and print none when no interface qualifies. Which three Jinja2 constructs are needed? (Choose three.)",,"A selectattr filter such as selectattr('role', 'equalto', 'access'), or an if test on the loop","The sort(attribute='name') filter","The else clause of the for loop","The upper filter","A macro that takes no arguments","The default('') filter applied to the whole list","Filtering keeps the access interfaces, sort orders them, and the else clause of a for loop runs when no iteration took place. upper changes case, a macro without arguments does not select anything, and default only replaces an undefined value.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.3|objective:cisco:350-901:1.1|topic:ansible","Which two statements about the cisco.nxos and cisco.dcnm Ansible collections are correct? (Choose two.)",,"cisco.nxos modules such as nxos_vlans manage one switch, typically over network_cli or the NX-API httpapi plugin","cisco.dcnm modules manage fabric objects such as VRFs and networks through the controller's REST API",,"cisco.dcnm modules connect to each switch over SSH","cisco.nxos modules require Nexus Dashboard to be installed","Both collections use the same ansible_network_os value","The nxos collection works device by device, while the dcnm collection works through the controller. They use different network OS values, and the nxos modules do not need a controller.",2,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.4|objective:cisco:350-901:1.2|topic:terraform","Which two benefits does terraform plan give before an apply against a fabric controller? (Choose two.)",,"It lists which resources would be created, changed or destroyed","It compares the configuration and the state with what the provider reads from the controller",,"It deploys the changes to a staging copy of the fabric","It makes the provider cache the credentials for later runs","It rewrites the configuration files to match the controller","A plan is a preview: Terraform refreshes what it knows, compares it with the configuration, and shows the actions it would take. It does not deploy anything, cache credentials or edit the configuration.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.5|objective:cisco:350-901:1.1|objective:cisco:300-435:2.4|topic:ansible|topic:troubleshooting","A playbook using cisco.nxos.nxos_vlans over network_cli reports authentication failures on some switches only. Which two checks are most useful? (Choose two.)",,"Check whether host or group variables override ansible_user or ansible_password for those switches","Log in manually with the same account and confirm that its role may run the module's commands",,"Increase the number of forks to 50","Change the connection to local so that Ansible runs on the control node","Disable fact gathering for all hosts","Failures on a subset of switches point to host-specific credentials or role differences. More forks, a local connection or disabling facts do not change how Ansible authenticates to those switches.",4,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.1|objective:cisco:300-435:2.2|topic:ncclient|topic:netconf","Which two ncclient calls can be used on a Nexus switch after manager.connect() succeeds? (Choose two.)",,"m.get_config(source='running', filter=...)","m.edit_config(target='running', config=...)",,"m.subscribe() to stream interface counters over gRPC","m.nxapi(cli='show version')","m.send_command('show version')","ncclient provides NETCONF operations such as get_config and edit_config against the running datastore. Streaming over gRPC, NX-API calls and send_command (a Netmiko method) are not ncclient manager methods.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.2|objective:cisco:300-435:2.5|topic:poap","Which two checks does a POAP script normally perform before it trusts the files it downloads? (Choose two.)",,"It compares the checksum of the software image with the one in the matching .md5 file","It compares the checksum of the configuration file with the one in the matching .md5 file",,"It validates the TLS certificate of the TFTP server","It compares the image version with the switch clock","It asks a controller to approve each file","The reference POAP script downloads an .md5 file for the image and for the configuration and verifies each file with it. TFTP has no TLS, the clock is irrelevant to integrity, and no controller approval step exists in the basic workflow.",2,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.3|objective:cisco:300-435:2.7|topic:python|topic:on-box","Which two statements about the cli module of on-box Python on NX-OS are correct? (Choose two.)",,"cli() returns the output of a command as a string","clid() returns the output of a show command as JSON text",,"cli() returns a list of parsed dictionaries","The interpreter works only after feature python is configured","clip() returns the command output as a string and prints nothing","After from cli import *, cli() returns the raw output string and clid() returns JSON where the command supports structured output. clip() prints the output and returns nothing, and no feature command is needed for the Python interpreter.",2,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.5|topic:ndfc|topic:templates","Which three section markers belong in a custom Nexus Dashboard Fabric Controller template? (Choose three.)",,"##template properties","##template variables","##template content","##template inventory","##template schedule","##template rollback","A template carries its properties (name, platforms, type), its variables and its content, each introduced by its own marker. Inventory, schedule and rollback are not template sections.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.6|topic:nxapi","Which two statements correctly distinguish NX-API REST from NX-API CLI? (Choose two.)",,"NX-API REST addresses managed objects of the switch's object model, such as paths under /api/mo/","NX-API CLI wraps CLI commands in a request and returns their output as structured JSON or XML",,"NX-API REST needs no feature to be enabled","NX-API CLI works only over SSH","Both use NETCONF as their transport","Both styles are served by NX-API after feature nxapi is enabled, over HTTP or HTTPS. REST works on the object model, and CLI works on commands. Neither uses SSH or NETCONF as its transport.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.7|topic:linux|topic:containers","Which two commands show which bridge the host-side veth end of a container is attached to? (Choose two.)",,"bridge link show, which lists bridge ports and the bridge each port belongs to","ip -d link show, which prints interface details such as the master device",,"ip route show table main, which lists routes but not bridge membership","ss -tulpn, which lists listening sockets","arp -a, which prints the neighbor cache","The bridge command and the detailed link output both expose the master of a port. Routes, sockets and neighbors do not describe bridge membership.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.6|topic:python|topic:nxapi","Which two sources can a Python health script query for a Nexus fabric? (Choose two.)",,"NX-API CLI on each switch, for show commands such as show system resources","The Nexus Dashboard REST API, after logging in to obtain a token",,"SNMP traps received on the script's loopback interface","The Terraform state file, as a live source of device health","A syslog file on the engineer's laptop","Health data comes from the devices through NX-API CLI and from the controller through its API. Traps need a receiver rather than a query, state files describe intended objects, and a laptop log is not fabric data.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.4|objective:cisco:300-435:4.5|objective:cisco:350-901:3.1|topic:telemetry|topic:gnmi","Which two statements about gNMI dial-in telemetry on NX-OS are true? (Choose two.)",,"The collector connects to the switch's gNMI service and sends a SubscribeRequest","The switch needs feature grpc, and the connection uses TLS with a certificate",,"The switch opens the connection to the collector","No certificate is used, because gNMI is always plaintext","It requires feature telemetry","In dial-in the client initiates the gRPC session to the switch, which serves gNMI after feature grpc is enabled and protects it with TLS. Dial-out is the model in which the switch connects out, and it uses feature telemetry.",2,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.2|objective:cisco:350-901:3.4|topic:pyats|topic:genie","Which two practices make pyATS change validation meaningful? (Choose two.)",,"Capture the pre-change snapshot before applying the change","Use the same testbed and the same features for the pre-change and post-change snapshots",,"Take only the post-change snapshot to save time","Learn different features before and after the change","Skip validation when the pipeline is green","A diff is only meaningful when both snapshots describe the same features on the same devices and the first one is taken before the change. A single snapshot cannot show a difference, and a green pipeline does not prove the network state.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.5|objective:cisco:350-901:2.6|topic:source-of-truth|topic:netbox","Which two data items belong in a network source of truth rather than being collected from devices? (Choose two.)",,"The VLAN IDs and names assigned to each site","The device roles and the intended cabling",,"Current interface error counters","Live BGP session states","CPU utilization of the last five minutes","Intended design data is authored once and used to generate configuration. Counters, session states and utilization are operational data that change constantly and come from the devices.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:5.3|objective:cisco:300-435:5.4|objective:cisco:350-901:4.3|topic:mcp|topic:ai-security","Which two design choices make an MCP server that exposes Nexus health data safer? (Choose two.)",,"Offer read-only tools by default and put any write tool behind explicit approval","Use a dedicated read-only account for the server's connection to the controller",,"Give the server a network-admin account so that it never meets permission errors","Return full running configurations to the model for context","Turn off logging so that no sensitive data is stored","Least privilege and approval for writes limit what a misled agent can do. A broad account increases the damage, full configurations leak secrets, and disabling logging removes the audit trail.",4,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:5.2|objective:cisco:300-435:5.3|objective:cisco:350-901:4.2|topic:ai|topic:ai-security","Which two are recognized security risks of an AI-based network automation solution? (Choose two.)",,"Prompt injection through data that the agent reads, such as syslog text","Excessive agency, where the agent holds broader write privileges than its task needs",,"Growth of the MAC address table","Loss of VXLAN encapsulation on the uplinks","Spanning tree loops caused by the model","Agents can be steered by instructions hidden in the data they process, and they can do harm when they hold more privileges than needed. The other options are network symptoms, not risks that come from using an AI agent.",2,2

match[20]{tags,text,exhibit,p1,r1,p2,r2,p3,r3,p4,r4,p5,r5,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.2|topic:aci","Match each ACI object to its role.",,"Tenant","Administrative container that isolates policy, objects and users","VRF","Layer 3 routing and forwarding context","Bridge domain","Layer 2 forwarding domain that holds the subnets used as gateways","EPG","Group of endpoints that share the same policy","Contract","Rules, built from subjects and filters, that permit traffic between EPGs","A tenant contains the policy objects, the VRF is the routing context, the bridge domain is the Layer 2 domain with its gateways, EPGs group endpoints, and contracts permit traffic between EPGs.",1,1
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.4|topic:gnmi|topic:netconf","Match each management interface to its transport and encoding.",,"NETCONF","SSH transport with XML-encoded RPCs","RESTCONF","HTTPS transport with resource-style requests in JSON or XML","gNMI","gRPC over HTTP/2 with protobuf messages","gNOI","gRPC operational RPCs such as reboot and file transfer","NX-API REST","HTTP or HTTPS requests addressed to managed objects under /api/mo","NETCONF runs over SSH, RESTCONF over HTTPS, and gNMI and gNOI over gRPC. NX-API REST is Cisco's object-model interface over HTTP(S).",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.1|objective:cisco:300-435:1.1|topic:yang","Match each YANG concept to its description.",,"OpenConfig","Vendor-neutral models written by network operators","IETF","Models standardized through RFCs, such as ietf-interfaces","Native","Models that expose all vendor-specific features of one platform","Deviation","Module that declares nodes a device does not implement or implements differently","Augment","Statement that adds nodes to the tree of an existing model","OpenConfig, IETF and native describe who writes a model and how far it reaches. Deviations and augments are mechanisms for adjusting or extending a model.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.1|objective:cisco:350-901:1.5|topic:iac","Match each infrastructure as code term to its meaning.",,"Declarative","You describe the desired end state and the tool works out the changes","Imperative","You script the exact steps to run in order","Idempotent","Running the same change twice leaves the same result","Drift","Difference between the intended state in the repository and what is on the devices","Source of truth","The authoritative record of intended state","Declarative tools plan from an end state, while imperative scripts list steps. Idempotence makes repeated runs safe, drift is a gap from intent, and the source of truth is where intent lives.",1,1
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.2|objective:cisco:300-435:3.3|topic:jinja2","Match each Jinja2 syntax element to its use.",,"{{ ... }}","Prints the value of an expression","{% ... %}","Control structure such as a for loop or an if test","{# ... #}","Comment that is not rendered","| upper","Filter that transforms a value","{% macro %}","Reusable named block that takes arguments","Double braces output values, brace-percent delimits statements, brace-hash is a comment, a pipe applies a filter, and a macro defines a reusable block.",1,1
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.3|objective:cisco:350-901:1.1|topic:ansible","Match each Ansible item to what it manages or does.",,"cisco.nxos.nxos_vlans","Manages VLANs on a single NX-OS switch","cisco.dcnm.dcnm_vrf","Adds or removes VRFs on a controller-managed VXLAN fabric","cisco.aci.aci_tenant","Manages a tenant on the APIC","cisco.mso.mso_schema","Manages schemas on Nexus Dashboard Orchestrator","ansible.netcommon.httpapi","Connection plugin that talks to a REST API","The collection name tells which platform a module targets. httpapi is the connection plugin used by controller collections that expose a REST API.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.4|objective:cisco:350-901:1.2|topic:terraform","Match each Terraform provider or concept to its target or role.",,"CiscoDevNet/nxos","NX-OS switches through NX-API REST","CiscoDevNet/aci","Objects of an APIC","CiscoDevNet/ndfc","Nexus Dashboard Fabric Controller","CiscoDevNet/mso","Nexus Dashboard Orchestrator, formerly MSO","State","Record of which real objects belong to which resources","Each provider is a plugin for one platform's API. State links the resources in the configuration to the objects that exist.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.5|objective:cisco:350-901:1.1|objective:cisco:350-901:1.2|topic:troubleshooting","Match each symptom to its most likely cause.",,"Ansible reports UNREACHABLE for one NX-OS host only","Wrong address or credentials for that host","Ansible cannot find cisco.dcnm.dcnm_vrf","The collection is not installed in the control node's environment","Terraform reports an error acquiring the state lock","Another run holds the lock, or an earlier run crashed","terraform plan wants to recreate every VLAN","The state no longer matches the resource addresses, for example after state was lost","A provider returns 401 or 403","The credentials are wrong or the account's role lacks rights","A host-specific failure points to that host's variables, a missing module points to a missing collection, and a lock error points to a concurrent or crashed run. Recreating everything means the state does not know the objects, and 401 or 403 is an authentication or authorization failure.",4,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.1|objective:cisco:300-435:2.2|topic:ncclient","Match each ncclient method to the NETCONF operation it performs.",,"get_config","Reads configuration from a datastore","edit_config","Changes configuration in a datastore","get","Retrieves configuration and operational state","dispatch","Sends a custom RPC","close_session","Ends the NETCONF session gracefully","The manager methods map to NETCONF operations: get-config, edit-config and get. dispatch sends any RPC, and close_session ends the session.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.2|objective:cisco:300-435:2.5|topic:poap","Match each stage of POAP to what happens in it.",,"DHCP discovery","The switch gets an address and the location of the script","Script download","The script is fetched and its checksum verified","Image installation","The image is downloaded, verified and installed when needed","Config replay","The staged configuration is applied after the switch reloads","Serial-number lookup","The script chooses a device-specific configuration file","POAP starts with DHCP, then runs the script, which can install an image and stage a configuration for replay after the reload. Scripts often pick a configuration file by serial number.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.3|objective:cisco:300-435:2.7|topic:on-box","Match each on-box NX-OS tool to its use.",,"Bash shell","Run Linux commands and scripts on the switch","Guest Shell","Isolated Linux container where packages can be installed","Python cli module","Run NX-OS CLI commands from a script on the switch","EEM applet","Run actions when an event such as a syslog pattern occurs","Scheduler","Run CLI jobs at set times","The Bash shell gives Linux access, Guest Shell is a separate container, the cli module is the API between Python and the CLI, EEM reacts to events, and the scheduler runs jobs on a timetable.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.4|topic:ndfc|topic:templates","Match each Nexus Dashboard Fabric Controller term to its meaning.",,"Template","Reusable CLI content with variables","Policy","A template instance with values, applied to switches","Freeform configuration","CLI added without a structured template","Template variable","Placeholder filled in when a policy is created","Deploy","Pushes the pending configuration to the switches","A template is the definition and a policy is the instance. Freeform text is unstructured CLI, variables are the placeholders, and deploy sends the pending configuration to the devices.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.6|topic:nxapi","Match each NX-API item to its meaning.",,"/ins","Endpoint of NX-API CLI requests","/api/mo/sys.json","Path that queries a managed object in NX-API REST","cli_show","Request type for show commands","cli_conf","Request type for configuration commands","feature nxapi","Command that turns the service on","/ins receives CLI-style requests, the /api/mo paths address objects, cli_show and cli_conf select the kind of commands, and feature nxapi enables the service.",1,1
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.7|topic:linux|topic:containers","Match each Linux networking element to its role.",,"veth pair","Virtual Ethernet link whose two ends can sit in different namespaces","Linux bridge","Software switch that learns MAC addresses","Bond interface","Aggregates physical NICs for redundancy or throughput","VLAN subinterface such as eth0.100","802.1Q interface carved out of a parent interface","Network namespace","Isolated network stack with its own interfaces and routes","A container usually reaches the network through a veth pair into a bridge, with bonds and VLAN subinterfaces on the uplink side and a namespace isolating the container's stack.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.3|objective:cisco:350-901:3.1|objective:cisco:300-435:4.5|topic:telemetry|topic:mdt","Match each telemetry component to its role.",,"Sensor path","Identifies the data to stream","Destination group","Receiver address, port, protocol and encoding","Subscription","Ties sensor groups to destination groups with a sample interval","Dial-out","The switch opens the connection to the receiver","Dial-in","The collector opens the connection to the switch","A subscription connects what to send with where to send it. Dial-out and dial-in describe which side starts the session.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.2|objective:cisco:350-901:3.4|topic:pyats|topic:genie","Match each pyATS term to its meaning.",,"Testbed","YAML description of the devices and how to connect","parse","Turns the output of one command into structured data","learn","Builds a feature model from several commands","diff","Compares two saved snapshots","Genie","pyATS library that provides parsers and feature models","The testbed names the devices, parse handles a single command, learn builds a feature view, diff compares results, and Genie supplies the parsers and models.",1,1
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.4|objective:cisco:350-901:3.1|topic:gnmi|topic:telemetry","Match each gNMI subscription mode to its behavior.",,"ONCE","Returns the current values and then ends","POLL","Returns values only when the client asks again","STREAM with SAMPLE","Sends values at a fixed interval","STREAM with ON_CHANGE","Sends an update only when a value changes","STREAM with TARGET_DEFINED","The device picks the mode for each path","The subscription mode decides whether data arrives once, on request, periodically, on change, or in the mode that the device prefers for each path.",1,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.1|objective:cisco:300-435:4.2|objective:cisco:350-901:2.4|topic:cml|topic:simulation","Match each simulation item to its meaning.",,"Cisco Modeling Labs","Platform that runs virtual network nodes in labs built through its API","virl2_client","Python library for CML lab lifecycle operations","Nexus 9000v","Virtual NX-OS node used in simulated topologies","Topology file","Lab definition that can be kept in Git","Digital twin","Simulated copy used to test changes before production","CML hosts the labs, virl2_client automates them, the virtual Nexus node supplies NX-OS behavior, and a topology file makes the lab repeatable. A digital twin is a simulated copy of the real network.",1,1
  "objective:cisco:300-635:5.2|objective:cisco:300-435:5.3|objective:cisco:350-901:4.2|topic:ai|topic:ai-security","Match each security risk of an AI-based automation solution to its mitigation.",,"Prompt injection","Treat tool output as untrusted data and filter or isolate it","Excessive agency","Least-privilege credentials and human approval for writes","Sensitive data disclosure","Redact secrets before data reaches the model","Hallucinated output","Validate against schemas and test in a lab","Malicious tool server","Use vetted servers and pin their versions","Each risk has its own control: untrusted input handling, least privilege and approval, redaction, validation before use, and supply-chain care for third-party tool servers.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:5.3|objective:cisco:300-435:5.4|objective:cisco:350-901:4.3|topic:mcp","Match each MCP concept to its meaning.",,"Tool","An action that the agent can invoke with arguments","Resource","Read-only context addressed by a URI","Prompt","Reusable instruction template","stdio transport","The server runs as a local subprocess and talks over standard streams","HTTP transport","The server is reachable over the network by remote clients","Tools act, resources give context, and prompts are templates. The transport decides whether the server is local or reachable over a network.",1,2

order[20]{tags,text,exhibit,s1,s2,s3,s4,s5,s6,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.5|objective:cisco:300-435:1.3|topic:yang|topic:pyang|topic:grpc","Place the steps of building a gNMI Set payload from a YANG module in the correct order.",,"Pick the YANG module and revision that the device supports","Print the tree with pyang -f tree to see the structure and node types","Identify the target path and any list keys","Write the JSON value that matches the node types","Send it in a gNMI Set request","Check the response and read the value back with Get","The module and its tree must be understood before the path and the value can be written. The request is sent last, and a read-back confirms the result.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.4|topic:gnmi|topic:grpc","Place the events of a gNMI dial-in streaming subscription in the correct order.",,"The client opens a TLS-protected gRPC channel to the switch's gNMI port","The client authenticates with its credentials","The client sends a SubscribeRequest with the paths and the SAMPLE mode","The switch returns the initial values","The switch sends a sync_response that marks the end of the initial values","The switch keeps sending updates at the sample interval","The channel and authentication come first, then the subscription. The switch sends the current values, signals that the initial set is complete, and continues with periodic updates.",2,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.2|topic:aci","Place the steps of building the policy for a simple web application in ACI in the correct order.",,"Create the tenant","Create the VRF in the tenant","Create the bridge domain and associate it with the VRF","Create the application profile and the EPG and associate the EPG with the bridge domain","Create the contract with its subject and filter","Make the EPGs provide and consume the contract","Each object depends on the one before it: a VRF in a tenant, a bridge domain in a VRF, an EPG in a bridge domain. The contract must exist before EPGs can provide or consume it.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.1|objective:cisco:350-901:2.1|objective:cisco:350-901:2.3|topic:gitops","Place the steps of a GitOps change to a data center network in the correct order.",,"A developer creates a branch and edits the data files","The developer opens a merge request","A reviewer approves it and the validation pipeline passes","The branch is merged into main","The pipeline deploys main to the fabric","Post-deployment checks confirm the intended state","A change starts on a branch, is reviewed and validated, merged, and only then deployed from main, followed by a check of the result.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.2|objective:cisco:300-435:3.3|topic:jinja2","Place the steps of generating device configuration with Jinja2 in the correct order.",,"Load the variable data from YAML or JSON","Create the Jinja2 environment and load the template","Render the template with the data","Review the generated configuration, for example as a diff","Push the rendered configuration to the device",,"Data and template come first, then rendering. The output is reviewed before anything is pushed to a device.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.3|objective:cisco:350-901:1.1|topic:ansible|topic:ndfc","Place the steps of running a cisco.dcnm playbook in the correct order.",,"Install the cisco.dcnm collection with ansible-galaxy","Define the controller in inventory with the httpapi connection and the network OS","Write the play with a dcnm_vrf task and state: merged","Run the playbook","Verify the VRF in Nexus Dashboard Fabric Controller",,"The collection must be installed and the controller defined before a play can use it. Verification in the controller comes after the run.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.4|objective:cisco:350-901:1.2|topic:terraform|topic:ndfc","Place the steps of managing a fabric with the CiscoDevNet/ndfc provider in the correct order.",,"Add the provider requirement to the configuration","Run terraform init","Declare the fabric resources","Run terraform plan with -out","Apply the saved plan","Check the fabric in Nexus Dashboard","The provider is declared and installed first, then the resources are described. A saved plan is reviewed and applied, and the controller confirms the result.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.5|objective:cisco:350-901:1.1|topic:ansible|topic:troubleshooting","Place the troubleshooting steps for an Ansible task that fails against a controller in the correct order.",,"Read the error message and note which task and host failed","Re-run the task with -vvv to see the request and the response","Reproduce the same call with curl or the controller's API page","Correct the variable, credential or payload","Re-run the playbook","Confirm that a second run reports no changes","The error identifies the task, verbose output shows the exchange, and a manual call isolates the cause. After the fix, the second run confirms idempotence.",4,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.1|objective:cisco:300-435:2.2|topic:ncclient|topic:netconf","Place the steps of an ncclient session with a Nexus switch in the correct order.",,"Import manager from ncclient","Call manager.connect() with the host, port, credentials and device_params","Send get_config or edit_config","Check the reply or catch an RPCError","Leave the with block so that the session closes",,"The library is imported, the session opens, operations are sent and checked, and the session closes when the context ends.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.2|objective:cisco:300-435:2.5|topic:poap|topic:day-0","Place the stages of POAP on a Nexus switch in the correct order.",,"The switch boots without a startup configuration and enters POAP","DHCP supplies an address, the script server and the script name","The switch downloads the script and verifies its checksum","The script downloads the image and the configuration","The switch reloads onto the new image","The staged configuration is replayed","POAP begins at boot, uses DHCP to find the script, and the script fetches the image and configuration. After the reload, the configuration is replayed.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.3|objective:cisco:300-435:2.7|topic:python|topic:on-box","Place the events of running an on-box Python script on an NX-OS switch in the correct order.",,"Copy the script to bootflash","Run it with the python command on the switch","The script imports the cli module","The script calls cli('show version')","The script parses the returned text","The script prints or saves the result","The file must be on the switch before it can run. Inside the script, the module is imported before it is used, and the output is parsed after the command returns.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.5|topic:ndfc|topic:templates","Place the steps of creating and using a custom Nexus Dashboard Fabric Controller template in the correct order.",,"Create a new template in the template library","Set the template properties such as name, type and content type","Declare the template variables","Write the CLI content using the variables","Validate the template","Create a policy from it and deploy it to the switches","A template is defined by its properties, variables and content, then validated. Only a validated template should be turned into a policy and deployed.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.6|topic:nxapi","Place the steps of sending a show command through NX-API CLI in the correct order.",,"Enable feature nxapi on the switch","Build an ins_api payload with type cli_show and the command as input","POST the payload to the switch's /ins endpoint with credentials","Read outputs.output.body from the JSON reply",,,"The service must be on first. The request is then built, sent to the /ins endpoint and read from the body of the reply.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.4|objective:cisco:300-435:4.5|objective:cisco:350-901:3.1|topic:telemetry|topic:grpc","Place the steps of configuring NX-OS dial-out telemetry in the correct order.",,"Enable feature telemetry","Create a sensor group and add the sensor path","Create a destination group with address, port, protocol and encoding","Create a subscription that ties them together with a sample interval","Verify that data arrives at the receiver",,"The feature comes first, then the sensor group and destination group, which the subscription refers to. Verification at the receiver comes last.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.2|objective:cisco:350-901:3.4|topic:pyats|topic:genie","Place the stages of a change-validation pipeline for a data center fabric in the correct order.",,"Create a branch and render the configuration","Run pyats learn to capture the baseline","Deploy the change","Run pyats learn again to capture the post-change state","Run pyats diff","Fail the pipeline if the differences are not expected","The baseline must be taken before the deployment. The post-change state is compared with it, and unexpected differences stop the pipeline.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.5|objective:cisco:350-901:2.6|topic:source-of-truth|topic:netbox","Place the steps of deploying a change from a source of truth in the correct order.",,"Update the device and VLAN records in the source of truth","The pipeline queries the source of truth API","Templates render the configuration of each device","The configuration is deployed","A compliance job compares intended and actual state","Drift is reported and corrected","Intent is recorded first, then read, rendered and deployed. A later comparison finds drift from the intent.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.7|topic:linux|topic:containers","Place the troubleshooting steps for a container that cannot reach a server in the correct order, starting inside the container.",,"Check the container's interface and address with ip addr in its namespace","Check the host-side veth peer and its master bridge","Check the bridge forwarding table with bridge fdb show","Check the state of the VLAN subinterface and bond on the uplink","Capture on the uplink to see whether the VLAN tag is present","Check the switch port and the VLAN on the leaf","Following the packet from the container outward isolates the failing hop: the interface, the veth and bridge, the forwarding table, the uplink, the capture, and finally the switch port.",4,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.6|topic:python|topic:nxapi","Place the steps of a Python health report for a fabric in the correct order.",,"Log in to Nexus Dashboard and keep the token","Query the list of switches","Call NX-API show system resources on each switch","Parse the replies and compare them with thresholds","Write the report",,"The script authenticates first, finds the switches, collects data from each, evaluates it and then writes the result.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:5.3|objective:cisco:350-901:4.4|objective:cisco:300-435:5.4|topic:mcp|topic:llm-agent","Place the events of an AI agent answering a question about a switch in the correct order.",,"The user asks which interfaces are down on a leaf","The application sends the message and the tool definitions to the model","The model returns a request to call a tool","The application runs the tool against the controller API","The application returns the tool result to the model","The model writes the final answer","The model can only request a tool, and the application executes it. The result goes back to the model, which then answers.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:5.1|objective:cisco:300-435:5.2|objective:cisco:350-901:4.1|objective:cisco:350-901:4.5|topic:ai|topic:code-validation","Place the steps of using an AI assistant safely to write network automation code in the correct order.",,"Write a prompt that includes the schema or API specification","Review the generated code","Lint it and run the unit tests","Run it in a lab or simulation","Merge it through a pull request","Deploy through the pipeline with post-validation","Context improves the first draft, and review and tests come before any device sees the code. Merging and deployment follow only after it passes in a safe environment.",3,2

slotfill[20]{tags,text,exhibit,a1,a2,a3,a4,a5,d1,d2,d3,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.4|topic:gnmi|topic:grpc","Fill in each blank so that gnmic reads one value once from the switch's gNMI service and accepts its self-signed certificate.","```bash\ngnmic -a 192.0.2.11:⟦1⟧ -u admin -p 'C1sco123' --⟦2⟧ ⟦3⟧ --path /System/bgp-items/inst-items/dom-items/Dom-list/rtrId\n```","50051","skip-verify","get",,,"830","insecure","subscribe","NX-OS serves gNMI on port 50051 by default, over TLS. --skip-verify accepts a self-signed certificate while still using TLS, and the get subcommand reads values once. Port 830 is NETCONF, --insecure would turn TLS off and fail against a TLS server, and subscribe opens a stream.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.5|objective:cisco:300-435:1.5|objective:cisco:300-435:1.4|topic:pyang|topic:yang","Fill in each blank so that pyang prints the text tree of ietf-interfaces and then an XML skeleton of its configuration data.","```bash\npyang -p yang/ -f ⟦1⟧ yang/ietf-interfaces.yang\npyang -p yang/ -f ⟦2⟧ --sample-xml-skeleton-doctype=config yang/ietf-interfaces.yang\n```","tree","sample-xml-skeleton",,,,"yin","depend","jstree","The tree format prints the RFC 8340 text tree, and sample-xml-skeleton produces an XML skeleton that the doctype option limits to configuration. yin converts the module to XML syntax, depend lists dependencies, and jstree produces an HTML tree.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:1.1|objective:cisco:300-435:1.1|topic:openconfig|topic:gnmi","Fill in each blank so that the gNMI Set updates the description of Ethernet1/1 through the OpenConfig model.","```bash\ngnmic -a 192.0.2.11:50051 -u admin -p 'C1sco123' --skip-verify set \\\n  --update-path /⟦1⟧/interface[name=Ethernet1/1]/⟦2⟧/description \\\n  --update-value uplink-to-spine\n```","interfaces","config",,,,"state","System","interface-items","In the OpenConfig interfaces model, writable values live under the config container and read-only values under state. System is the root of the NX-OS native tree, and interface-items belongs to the native model.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.1|objective:cisco:350-901:2.3|objective:cisco:350-901:2.1|topic:gitops|topic:gitlab-ci","Fill in each blank so that the merge request pipeline previews the change without applying it.","```yaml\nvalidate-change:\n  stage: test\n  script:\n    - ansible-playbook site.yml --⟦1⟧ --diff\n  rules:\n    - if: '$CI_PIPELINE_SOURCE == \"⟦2⟧\"'\n```","check","merge_request_event",,,,"syntax-check","push","schedule","--check runs the playbook without changing devices, and --diff shows the lines that would change. merge_request_event is the pipeline source of merge request pipelines. --syntax-check only parses the playbook, and push or schedule sources do not match merge requests.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.2|objective:cisco:300-435:3.3|topic:jinja2","Fill in each blank so that the template prints a description only when the interface has one.","```jinja\n{% ⟦1⟧ intf in interfaces %}\ninterface {{ intf.name }}\n{% ⟦2⟧ intf.description is defined %}\n  description {{ intf.description }}\n{% ⟦3⟧ %}\n{% endfor %}\n```","for","if","endif",,,"macro","set","elif","A for statement loops over the interfaces, an if statement guards the description, and endif closes the guard. macro and set do not test a condition, and elif belongs inside an if block.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.3|objective:cisco:350-901:1.1|topic:ansible|topic:nexus-dashboard","Fill in each blank so that Ansible connects to Nexus Dashboard through its REST API.","```yaml\nnd:\n  hosts:\n    nd1:\n      ansible_host: 192.0.2.50\n  vars:\n    ansible_connection: ansible.netcommon.⟦1⟧\n    ansible_network_os: cisco.⟦2⟧.nd\n    ansible_httpapi_use_ssl: true\n    ansible_httpapi_validate_certs: false\n```","httpapi","nd",,,,"network_cli","netconf","dcnm","The cisco.nd collection uses the httpapi connection plugin and the network OS cisco.nd.nd. network_cli and netconf are CLI and NETCONF transports, and dcnm names the controller collection instead.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.4|objective:cisco:350-901:1.2|topic:terraform|topic:ndfc","Fill in each blank so that Terraform uses the Nexus Dashboard Fabric Controller provider and accepts the controller's self-signed certificate.","```hcl\nterraform {\n  required_providers {\n    ndfc = {\n      source = \"⟦1⟧\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nprovider \"ndfc\" {\n  username = \"admin\"\n  password = var.password\n  url      = \"https://192.0.2.50\"\n  ⟦2⟧     = true\n}\n```","CiscoDevNet/ndfc","insecure",,,,"CiscoDevNet/aci","hashicorp/ndfc","skip_verify","The provider is published as CiscoDevNet/ndfc, and its insecure argument allows a certificate that is not signed by a trusted authority. CiscoDevNet/aci targets an APIC, and hashicorp does not publish this provider.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:2.5|objective:cisco:350-901:1.2|topic:terraform|topic:troubleshooting","Fill in each blank to release a stale lock, bring a hand-made VRF under Terraform control, and list what the state manages.","```bash\nterraform ⟦1⟧ 5c7a1f0e-3b2d-4c8e-9a11-0d2f6b7e8a90\nterraform ⟦2⟧ ndfc_vrf.prod 'fabric1/prod'\nterraform state ⟦3⟧\n```","force-unlock","import","list",,,"refresh","taint","show","force-unlock releases a lock by its ID, import attaches an existing object to a resource address, and state list prints the addresses in the state. refresh and taint do not release locks or import objects, and state show needs an address.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.1|objective:cisco:300-435:2.2|topic:ncclient|topic:netconf","Fill in each blank so that ncclient connects to a Nexus switch and reads its running configuration.","```python\nfrom ncclient import manager\n\ndef read_running(host):\n    # Purpose: return the running configuration of one Nexus switch over NETCONF\n    with manager.connect(host=host, port=⟦1⟧, username='admin', password='C1sco123',\n                         hostkey_verify=False, device_params={'name': '⟦2⟧'}) as m:\n        return m.⟦3⟧(source='running').xml\n```","830","nexus","get_config",,,"443","iosxe","edit_config","NX-OS listens for NETCONF on port 830, ncclient has a nexus device handler, and get_config reads a datastore. Port 443 is HTTPS, the iosxe handler is for IOS XE, and edit_config changes configuration.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.2|objective:cisco:300-435:2.5|topic:poap|topic:day-0","Fill in each blank so that the DHCP pool gives a POAP switch the TFTP server address and the script name.","```text\nip dhcp pool poap-pool\n network 10.20.0.0 255.255.255.0\n default-router 10.20.0.1\n option ⟦1⟧ ip 10.20.0.5\n option ⟦2⟧ ascii \"poap.py\"\n```","150","67",,,,"43","66","121","Option 150 carries the TFTP server address and option 67 the boot file name, here the POAP script. Option 43 is vendor-specific data, option 66 would take the server as text rather than an IP list, and option 121 carries classless routes.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.3|objective:cisco:300-435:2.7|topic:bash|topic:on-box","Fill in each blank to enable the Bash shell on a Nexus switch and open it.","```text\nswitch(config)# feature ⟦1⟧\nswitch(config)# end\nswitch# run ⟦2⟧\n```","bash-shell","bash",,,,"guestshell","scheduler","nxapi","feature bash-shell enables the shell, and run bash opens it. Guest Shell is enabled differently, and the scheduler and NX-API are unrelated to shell access.",2,1
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.5|topic:ndfc|topic:templates","Fill in each blank with the section marker that the content below it needs.","```text\n##template ⟦1⟧\nname = banner_policy;\ndescription = Login banner;\nsupportedPlatforms = All;\ntemplateType = POLICY;\ncontentType = TEMPLATE_CLI;\n##\n##template ⟦2⟧\nstring BANNER_TEXT;\n##\n##template ⟦3⟧\nbanner motd #$$BANNER_TEXT$$#\n##\n```","properties","variables","content",,,"inventory","schedule","rollback","A template begins with its properties, declares its variables, and ends with the content that uses them. Inventory, schedule and rollback are not template sections.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:3.6|topic:nxapi","Fill in each blank so that the request runs a show command through NX-API CLI and asks for JSON.","```python\nimport requests\n\ndef show_interfaces(host):\n    # Purpose: run show interface brief through NX-API CLI and return the JSON reply\n    payload = {'ins_api': {'version': '1.0', 'type': '⟦1⟧', 'chunk': '0', 'sid': '1',\n                           'input': 'show interface brief', 'output_format': '⟦2⟧'}}\n    r = requests.post(f'https://{host}/⟦3⟧', json=payload, auth=('admin', 'C1sco123'), verify=False)\n    return r.json()\n```","cli_show","json","ins",,,"cli_conf","xml","api","cli_show is the type for show commands, the output format json asks for JSON, and NX-API CLI requests go to /ins. cli_conf is for configuration, xml would return XML, and /api is not the CLI endpoint.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.4|objective:cisco:300-435:4.5|objective:cisco:350-901:3.1|topic:telemetry|topic:grpc","Fill in each blank to build the dial-out telemetry configuration.","```text\ntelemetry\n  destination-group 1\n    ip address 192.0.2.50 port 57000 protocol ⟦1⟧ encoding ⟦2⟧\n  sensor-group 1\n    path sys/intf\n  subscription 1\n    ⟦3⟧ 1\n    ⟦4⟧ 1 sample-interval 60000\n```","gRPC","GPB","dst-grp","snsr-grp",,"UDP","XML","sensor-grp","A destination group names the receiver with protocol gRPC and encoding GPB, and the subscription refers to the groups with dst-grp and snsr-grp. UDP and XML are not valid here, and sensor-grp is not the keyword.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.4|topic:telemetry|topic:gnmi","Fill in each blank to enable the gNMI service on port 50051 with a trustpoint, reachable from the default VRF.","```text\nswitch(config)# feature ⟦1⟧\nswitch(config)# grpc ⟦2⟧ 50051\nswitch(config)# grpc ⟦3⟧ GNMI-TP\nswitch(config)# grpc ⟦4⟧ default\n```","grpc","port","certificate","use-vrf",,"telemetry","vrf","trustpoint","feature grpc enables the agent, grpc port sets the port, grpc certificate names the trustpoint, and grpc use-vrf default lets the service answer on the default VRF as well as the management VRF.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.7|topic:linux|topic:containers","Fill in each blank to create a veth pair, attach the host end to the bridge br0, and create the VLAN 120 subinterface on bond0.","```bash\nip link add veth-c0 type ⟦1⟧ peer name veth-h0\nip link set veth-h0 master ⟦2⟧\nip link add link bond0 name bond0.120 type vlan id ⟦3⟧\n```","veth","br0","120",,,"bridge","vlan","100","A veth pair is created with type veth, the host end joins the bridge with master br0, and a VLAN subinterface uses the VLAN ID that matches its name. Type bridge would create a bridge, and 100 would tag the wrong VLAN.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.5|objective:cisco:350-901:2.6|topic:source-of-truth|topic:netbox","Fill in each blank so that the function returns the VLAN IDs of one site from NetBox.","```python\nimport pynetbox\n\ndef site_vlans(url, token, site):\n    # Purpose: return the VLAN IDs of one site from the source of truth\n    nb = pynetbox.api(url, token=token)\n    return [v.vid for v in nb.ipam.⟦1⟧.⟦2⟧(site=site)]\n```","vlans","filter",,,,"prefixes","get","all","nb.ipam.vlans is the VLAN endpoint, and filter returns every record that matches the criteria. get returns exactly one record and raises an error when several match, prefixes is another endpoint, and all returns every VLAN in NetBox.",3,3
  "objective:cisco:300-635:4.6|topic:python|topic:nexus-dashboard","Fill in each blank so that the request logs in to Nexus Dashboard.","```python\nimport requests\n\ndef nd_login(host, user, password):\n    # Purpose: log in to Nexus Dashboard and return the login reply\n    r = requests.post(f'https://{host}/⟦1⟧', json={'userName': user, '⟦2⟧': password, '⟦3⟧': 'local'}, verify=False)\n    return r.json()\n```","login","userPasswd","domain",,,"auth","password","realm","The documented login call is a POST to /login with userName, userPasswd and domain in the JSON body. The reply carries the token that later requests present.",3,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:5.3|objective:cisco:300-435:5.4|objective:cisco:350-901:4.3|topic:mcp","Fill in each blank so that an MCP client starts the server as a local subprocess.","```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"nexus\": {\n      \"⟦1⟧\": \"python\",\n      \"⟦2⟧\": [\"nexus_server.py\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```","command","args",,,,"run","script","exec","An MCP client entry names the executable in command and its arguments in args, and then talks to the process over standard streams. The other keys are not used for this.",2,2
  "objective:cisco:300-635:5.2|objective:cisco:300-435:5.3|objective:cisco:350-901:4.2|topic:ai|topic:ai-security","Fill in each blank so that the function replaces the value after password or secret with a marker and keeps the keyword.","```python\nimport re\n\ndef redact(config):\n    # Purpose: mask secrets in configuration text before it reaches an AI model\n    return re.⟦1⟧(r'(password|secret) \\S+', r'⟦2⟧ <redacted>', config)\n```","sub","\\1",,,,"match","findall","\\2","re.sub replaces every match, and the group reference \\1 keeps the matched keyword. match tests only the start of a string, findall returns matches without replacing them, and \\2 refers to a group that does not exist.",3,3
